\section{Qualitative Analysis}
\label{sec:qualitative}

We present representative examples illustrating three phenomena: evidence quality differences between SFT and RSFT, GRPO format gaming under catastrophic collapse, and per-relation EDCR diagnostics.

\paragraph{Case 1: Evidence quality.}
Table~\ref{tab:case_evidence} compares evidence citations from SFT and RSFT on three documents. SFT tends to cite many sentences indiscriminately, including distractors unrelated to the target triple, while RSFT selects precisely the gold evidence sentences. For \emph{America's Sweetheart}, SFT cites 6 sentences (precision 0.17) whereas RSFT cites only the single correct sentence (precision 1.00). The pattern is consistent: SFT achieves evidence precision of 0.00--0.20 across these examples, while RSFT achieves 1.00 in all three cases.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Evidence citation comparison between SFT and RSFT. Precision measures the fraction of cited sentences that match gold evidence.}
\label{tab:case_evidence}
\small
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{llccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Document} & \textbf{Triple} & \textbf{SFT Prec.} & \textbf{RSFT Prec.} & \textbf{SFT \#Cited} \\
\midrule
Oleg Tinkov & (Oleg Tinkov, country of citizenship, Russia) & 0.00 & 1.00 & 3 \\
America's Sweetheart & (record label, country, United States) & 0.17 & 1.00 & 6 \\
Jade (Mortal Kombat) & (Jade, present in work, Mortal Kombat) & 0.20 & 1.00 & 5 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}%
}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Case 2: GRPO format gaming.}
Under catastrophic collapse (GRPO QLoRA $G{=}4$), the model abandons the DocRED 96-type relation ontology and generates free-text relation labels. On the document \emph{Something to Believe In}, the model produces 35 predicted triples with relations such as ``is a'', ``honor'', and ``location'', none of which belong to the target ontology. All 35 predictions are incorrect. Similarly, for \emph{J\'{o}zef Pi\l{}sudski Monument}, 28 predictions use non-standard relations with 0 correct matches. The model retains the structural format of triple-with-evidence output but fills it with fabricated relation types, producing well-formed evidence for non-existent relations.

\paragraph{Case 3: EDCR diagnostics.}
Per-relation EDCR identifies specific relation types where extraction and evidence are misaligned. For \texttt{date\_of\_birth} (EDCR $= 0.118$), the model's evidence consistently matches gold annotations: both extraction and evidence point to the same biographical sentence. In contrast, \texttt{notable\_work} (EDCR $= 0.940$) exhibits severe decoupling: the model cites sentence [0] (a general introduction) instead of the gold evidence sentence [3] (which contains the actual work attribution). High-EDCR relations tend to involve indirect or multi-hop reasoning where surface-level context misleads the evidence generator.

