\subsection{Generalization}
\label{sec:generalization}

Cross-architecture validation on LLaMA-3.1-8B confirms the scaling patterns: the evidence tax at 8B is near zero ($-0.68$ points), and RSFT CED yields +1.74 Ign-F1 gain. The RSFT improvement transfers consistently across datasets: on Re-DocRED~\citep{tan2022redocred}, RSFT achieves 41.45 Ign-F1 (+4.86pp over SFT baseline 36.59), closely matching the +4.92pp gain on DocRED. Evidence quality remains stable (Evi-F1 81.82 vs.\ 81.70 for SFT) with improved coupling (EDCR 0.6210 vs.\ 0.6674). Our best joint Evi-F1 (0.36, DREEAM-protocol denominator) falls below supervised DREEAM (54.01\%~\citep{ma2023dreeam}), a gap driven by extraction performance rather than evidence quality (TP-only Evi-F1 exceeds 80\%). Full results appear in Appendix~\ref{sec:generalization_full}.

\section{Limitations}

All results use the DocRED development set; test set submission is pending. Exact-match F1 penalizes surface-form-different entity mentions~\citep{wan2024genres}, likely underestimating absolute extraction capability, though relative comparisons remain valid.

We evaluate one algorithm per RL family (GRPO, DPO); other algorithms may yield different results. CED scoring depends on an external NLI model, and the RSFT ablation uses limited seeds (four for CED, two for flat-NLI). The evidence tax pattern observed up to 8B may not extrapolate to larger scales. At 1.7B scale, 44.4\% of outputs exhibit repetitive patterns (vs.\ negligible at 4B+); small models may require decoding-time interventions (e.g., repetition penalty) for reliable deployment.

